@startuml
title Flux de commande asynchrone - Architecture Microservices

actor "Client" as client
participant "Command Service\n:8084" as cmd
participant "Thread Pool" as pool
participant "Command Store\n(in-memory)" as store
participant "Capteur distant\n(simulé)" as sensor

== Envoi de commande (non-bloquant) ==
client -> cmd: POST /api/commands\n{sensorId: "TEMP-001", command: "READ_VALUE"}
activate cmd

cmd -> cmd: Valider requête
cmd -> store: Créer commande\nid=UUID, status=PENDING
cmd -> pool: CompletableFuture.runAsync()\nprocessCommand(command)
activate pool

cmd --> client: 202 Accepted\nLocation: /api/commands/{id}\n{commandId, status: "PENDING"}
deactivate cmd
note right of client: Client reçoit immédiatement\nune réponse (non bloqué)

== Traitement asynchrone (dans thread séparé) ==
pool -> sensor: Envoyer commande\n(simulation 2-5s)
activate sensor
note right: Thread.sleep(2000-5000)\nSimulation réseau + traitement
sensor --> pool: Réponse\n(80% succès, 20% échec)
deactivate sensor

pool -> store: Mettre à jour commande\nstatus=SUCCESS/FAILED\nresponse="Valeur: 23.5°C"
deactivate pool

== Polling du client (quelques secondes plus tard) ==
client -> cmd: GET /api/commands/{id}
activate cmd
cmd -> store: Récupérer commande
store --> cmd: Command{id, status, response}
cmd --> client: 200 OK\n{id, sensorId, command, status: "SUCCESS",\nresponse: "Valeur: 23.5°C", sentAt}
deactivate cmd

note left of client: Client obtient le résultat\nvia polling périodique

@enduml